Added the consent screen, and proved the whole flow against a third-party OAuth client. `@@oauth-authorize` now asks the user before issuing a code, and remembers the answer per user and client: the prompt appears the first time and again whenever a client comes back for a scope not already agreed to, so an existing session at the authorization server keeps meaning something. Consent is recorded as the whole scope list the user was actually shown rather than merged into what they agreed to before — merging is how a consent screen ends up recording more than anybody said yes to. The screen sits at the *end* of validation, never the start: a user is never shown a form approving a request that was going to be refused anyway, and a form rendered before the redirect URI is verified would be a phishing page this server hosts on the client's behalf. It is a page template so a site can restyle or override it, standalone rather than wrapped in `main_template` because the relying party is waiting for the browser back and site navigation invites the user to wander off mid-flow. The form carries the entire authorization request back as hidden fields rather than parking it in a session, so there is no server-side state to expire between the question and the answer, and the POST re-runs every check the GET did — a client disabled while the user was reading is refused on the way out. A consent POST without a valid `plone.protect` token raises `Forbidden` rather than being treated as a denial: forging one is an attempt to authorize an application in somebody else's name, and it should look like the attack it is. Denial reports `access_denied` to the client with the `state` echoed. The gate check runs `authlib`'s own `OAuth2Session` — the same library this package uses as a *client*, pointed the other way and knowing nothing about this package — through authorization, PKCE, consent, code redemption and a Bearer-authenticated request to Plone, plus the refusal of a replayed code. There is still no way to withdraw consent; that belongs with the account screen listing what somebody has agreed to, and neither exists in v1. @ericof
